<Weblab2>
  <config><![CDATA[{
  "game_id": 74,
  "created_at": "2026-05-28T16:59:18.000Z",
  "level_num": "custom",
  "user_id": 574,
  "properties": {
    "require_edit_to_continue": true,
    "start_sources": {
      "folders": {
      },
      "files": {
        "7746a3f8-ef1b-41a7-9c1e-5bf6bc5c0d3d": {
          "id": "7746a3f8-ef1b-41a7-9c1e-5bf6bc5c0d3d",
          "name": "index.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <title>3D Materials & Lighting</title>\n    <style>\n        body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; min-height: 100vh; background-color: #333; font-family: sans-serif; color: #eee; }\n        canvas { display: block; border: 2px solid #555; background-color: #000; }\n        h1 { position: absolute; top: 20px; color: #fff; text-align: center; width: 100%; }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h1>3D Materials & Lighting</h1>\n    <canvas id=\"threeJsCanvas\"></canvas>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": true
        },
        "b1c5000f-bbc2-4077-886d-bb88d9b0b0e8": {
          "id": "b1c5000f-bbc2-4077-886d-bb88d9b0b0e8",
          "name": "script.js",
          "contents": "// This script simulates a basic 3D scene setup for demonstrating lighting and materials.\n// It provides a simplified API resembling Three.js for educational purposes.\n// The actual 3D rendering is handled by the Web Lab environment.\n\n// --- Scene Setup (Simulated) ---\nconst scene = {\n    children: [], // Where objects and lights are added\n    add: function(object) {\n        this.children.push(object);\n        console.log(`Added object to scene:`, object);\n    }\n};\n\nconst camera = {\n    position: { x: 0, y: 1, z: 5 }, // Slightly above the scene\n    lookAt: { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 }\n};\n\nconst renderer = {\n    domElement: null, // Will be set after document is ready\n    setSize: function(width, height) {\n        if (this.domElement) {\n            this.domElement.width = width;\n            this.domElement.height = height;\n        }\n        console.log(`Renderer size set to ${width}x${height}`);\n    },\n    render: function(scene, camera) {\n        // This is a *highly simplified* 2D representation for visualization.\n        // In a real 3D engine, much more complex rendering would occur based on lights.\n        console.log(\"---\" + (new Date()).toLocaleTimeString() + \" Rendering Scene ---\");\n        const ctx = this.domElement.getContext('2d');\n        const width = this.domElement.width;\n        const height = this.domElement.height;\n\n        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);\n        ctx.fillStyle = '#333'; // Dark background\n        ctx.fillRect(0, 0, width, height);\n\n        const scale = 50; // Pixels per unit\n        const offsetX = width / 2;\n        const offsetY = height / 2;\n\n        // Draw objects\n        scene.children.filter(obj => obj.type === 'Mesh').forEach(obj => {\n            ctx.save();\n            ctx.translate(offsetX + obj.position.x * scale, offsetY - obj.position.y * scale); // Y-axis inverted for canvas vs 3D\n            ctx.fillStyle = obj.material.color;\n            ctx.strokeStyle = '#fff';\n            ctx.lineWidth = 1;\n\n            if (obj.geometry.type === 'BoxGeometry') {\n                ctx.fillRect(-25, -25, 50, 50); // Centered box\n                ctx.strokeRect(-25, -25, 50, 50);\n\n                // Simulate highlight based on roughness\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) { // Very shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff'; // Bright highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(15, -20, 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) { // Moderately shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)'; // Fainter highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(10, -15, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            } else if (obj.geometry.type === 'SphereGeometry') {\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 30, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                // Simulate highlight based on roughness\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) { // Very shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff'; // Bright highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(15, -15, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) { // Moderately shiny\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)'; // Fainter highlight\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(10, -10, 12, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            } else if (obj.geometry.type === 'ConeGeometry') {\n                const coneHeight = obj.geometry.height * scale;\n                const coneRadius = obj.geometry.radius * scale;\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.moveTo(0, -coneHeight / 2); // Top point\n                ctx.lineTo(-coneRadius, coneHeight / 2); // Bottom left\n                ctx.lineTo(coneRadius, coneHeight / 2); // Bottom right\n                ctx.closePath();\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(0, -10, 5, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(0, -5, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            } else if (obj.geometry.type === 'CylinderGeometry') {\n                const cylHeight = obj.geometry.height * scale;\n                const cylRadius = obj.geometry.radiusTop * scale; // Assuming radiusTop == radiusBottom\n                const ellipseYOffset = cylHeight / 2;\n                const ellipseHeightFactor = 0.3; // Make it look elliptical\n\n                // Draw the body (rectangle)\n                ctx.fillRect(-cylRadius, -ellipseYOffset, cylRadius * 2, cylHeight);\n                ctx.strokeRect(-cylRadius, -ellipseYOffset, cylRadius * 2, cylHeight);\n\n                // Draw the top ellipse\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.ellipse(0, -ellipseYOffset, cylRadius, cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor, 0, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                // Draw the bottom ellipse\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.ellipse(0, ellipseYOffset, cylRadius, cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor, 0, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n\n                if (obj.material.roughness < 0.3) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(cylRadius * 0.7, -ellipseYOffset - (cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor / 2), 6, 0, Math.PI * 2); // Highlight on top face\n                    ctx.fill();\n                } else if (obj.material.roughness < 0.7) {\n                    ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5)';\n                    ctx.beginPath();\n                    ctx.arc(cylRadius * 0.5, -ellipseYOffset - (cylRadius * ellipseHeightFactor / 3), 10, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                    ctx.fill();\n                }\n            }\n            ctx.restore();\n        });\n\n        // Draw lights (simplified indicators)\n        scene.children.filter(obj => obj.type && obj.type.includes('Light')).forEach(obj => {\n            ctx.save();\n            ctx.fillStyle = obj.color;\n            ctx.strokeStyle = '#fff';\n            ctx.lineWidth = 1;\n\n            if (obj.type === 'PointLight' && obj.position) {\n                ctx.translate(offsetX + obj.position.x * scale, offsetY - obj.position.y * scale);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('P', -3, 3); // Label for Point Light\n            } else if (obj.type === 'DirectionalLight' && obj.direction) {\n                // Indicate a directional light from a corner, with an arrow\n                ctx.translate(width * 0.15, height * 0.15);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('D', -3, 3); // Label for Directional Light\n                // Draw an arrow indicating direction\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.moveTo(0, 0);\n                // Simplify direction representation for 2D. Let's just point from origin of marker\n                const arrowLength = 20;\n                ctx.lineTo(obj.direction.x * arrowLength, -obj.direction.y * arrowLength); // Y inverted\n                ctx.strokeStyle = obj.color;\n                ctx.stroke();\n            } else if (obj.type === 'AmbientLight') {\n                // Indicate ambient light with a larger circle or fill\n                ctx.translate(width * 0.85, height * 0.15);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('A', -3, 3); // Label for Ambient Light\n            }\n            ctx.restore();\n        });\n    }\n};\n\n// --- Helper for Vector-like operations (simulated) ---\nfunction Vector3(x = 0, y = 0, z = 0) {\n    this.x = x;\n    this.y = y;\n    this.z = z;\n}\nVector3.prototype.set = function(x, y, z) {\n    this.x = x;\n    this.y = y;\n    this.z = z;\n    return this;\n};\nVector3.prototype.normalize = function() {\n    const length = Math.sqrt(this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y + this.z * this.z);\n    if (length > 0) {\n        this.x /= length;\n        this.y /= length;\n        this.z /= length;\n    }\n    return this;\n};\n\n\n// --- Geometry and Material (Simulated) ---\nfunction BoxGeometry(width, height, depth) {\n    this.type = 'BoxGeometry';\n    this.width = width; this.height = height; this.depth = depth;\n}\n\nfunction SphereGeometry(radius, widthSegments, heightSegments) {\n    this.type = 'SphereGeometry';\n    this.radius = radius;\n}\n\nfunction ConeGeometry(radius, height, radialSegments) {\n    this.type = 'ConeGeometry';\n    this.radius = radius;\n    this.height = height;\n}\n\nfunction CylinderGeometry(radiusTop, radiusBottom, height, radialSegments) {\n    this.type = 'CylinderGeometry';\n    this.radiusTop = radiusTop;\n    this.radiusBottom = radiusBottom;\n    this.height = height;\n}\n\nfunction MeshStandardMaterial(parameters) {\n    this.type = 'MeshStandardMaterial';\n    // Convert Three.js-like hex color to CSS color string\n    this.color = parameters.color ? `#${(parameters.color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}` : '#ffffff';\n    this.roughness = parameters.roughness !== undefined ? parameters.roughness : 0.5; // Default mid-roughness\n    this.metalness = parameters.metalness !== undefined ? parameters.metalness : 0.0; // Default non-metal\n}\n\nfunction Mesh(geometry, material) {\n    this.type = 'Mesh';\n    this.geometry = geometry;\n    this.material = material;\n    this.position = new Vector3();\n    this.rotation = new Vector3(); // For completeness\n}\n\n// --- Light Types (Simulated) ---\n// Students used these constructors in the previous level\nfunction AmbientLight(color = 0x404040, intensity = 1) { // Default to a dim grey\n    this.type = 'AmbientLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`; // Convert hex to css color\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n}\n\nfunction PointLight(color = 0xffffff, intensity = 1, distance = 0, decay = 1) {\n    this.type = 'PointLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`;\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n    this.position = new Vector3();\n}\n\nfunction DirectionalLight(color = 0xffffff, intensity = 1) {\n    this.type = 'DirectionalLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`;\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n    // In Three.js, direction is implied by position vs. target.\n    // Here, we'll give it an explicit 'direction' vector for simplicity.\n    this.direction = new Vector3(0, -1, 0); // Default to shining down\n}\n\n// --- Initialize Scene ---\nfunction init() {\n    renderer.domElement = document.getElementById('threeJsCanvas');\n    if (!renderer.domElement) {\n        console.error(\"Canvas element not found!\");\n        return;\n    }\n    renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth * 0.8, window.innerHeight * 0.8); // Adjust size for better fit\n\n    // Create objects with initial materials for students to modify\n    const cube = new Mesh(\n        new BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xff0000, roughness: 0.1, metalness: 0.9 }) // Shiny metallic red\n    );\n    cube.position.set(-1.5, 0, 0); // Position the cube to the left\n    scene.add(cube);\n\n    const sphere = new Mesh(\n        new SphereGeometry(0.7, 32, 32),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x0000ff, roughness: 0.8, metalness: 0.0 }) // Dull plastic blue\n    );\n    sphere.position.set(1.5, 0, 0); // Position the sphere to the right\n    scene.add(sphere);\n\n    const cone = new Mesh(\n        new ConeGeometry(0.7, 1.5, 32),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x00ff00, roughness: 0.4, metalness: 0.2 }) // Moderately shiny green\n    );\n    cone.position.set(0, 0.75, -2); // Position further back\n    scene.add(cone);\n\n    const cylinder = new Mesh(\n        new CylinderGeometry(0.5, 0.5, 1, 32),\n        new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xffff00, roughness: 0.9, metalness: 0.0 }) // Very rough yellow\n    );\n    cylinder.position.set(-2, 0.5, 1); // Position to the left-front\n    scene.add(cylinder);\n\n    // --- Lights from the previous level (already configured) ---\n    const ambientLight = new AmbientLight(0x404040, 0.5);\n    scene.add(ambientLight);\n\n    const pointLight = new PointLight(0xffffff, 1);\n    pointLight.position.set(0, 2, 2);\n    scene.add(pointLight);\n\n    const directionalLight = new DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.8);\n    directionalLight.direction.set(1, 1, 1).normalize();\n    scene.add(directionalLight);\n    // --- End of Lights ---\n\n    // Initial render based on the current scene state\n    renderer.render(scene, camera);\n}\n\n// This function would typically be an animation loop in a real 3D application.\n// For this lesson, we only need to render once after setup.\nfunction animate() {\n    // requestAnimationFrame(animate);\n    // renderer.render(scene, camera);\n}\n\n// Initialize the scene when the script loads\ninit();",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "7746a3f8-ef1b-41a7-9c1e-5bf6bc5c0d3d",
        "b1c5000f-bbc2-4077-886d-bb88d9b0b0e8"
      ]
    },
    "long_instructions": "You've successfully added lights to your 3D scene! Now, let's explore how different **materials** can dramatically change an object's appearance and how it reacts to those lights.\r\n\r\n1.  **Open `script.js`:** Find the sections where `MeshStandardMaterial` is used to create materials for the `cube`, `sphere`, `cone`, and `cylinder`.\r\n2.  **Adjust `color`:** Experiment with the `color` property (e.g., `0xff0000` for red, `0x00ff00` for green, `0x0000ff` for blue) on different objects. Remember, these are hexadecimal color codes!\r\n3.  **Change `roughness`:** The `roughness` property determines how shiny or dull an object appears. A value of `0` is perfectly smooth and shiny, while `1` is completely rough and matte. Try changing the `roughness` for the `cube` to `0.1` (very shiny) and then to `0.9` (very rough). Observe how the 'highlight' on the object changes.\r\n4.  **Experiment with `metalness`:** The `metalness` property makes an object look more like metal. A value of `0` is non-metallic (like plastic or wood), and `1` is fully metallic. Try setting the `sphere`'s `metalness` to `1.0` and its `roughness` to `0.2` to create a shiny metallic ball. Note that in this simplified 2D renderer, the visual change for `metalness` might be subtle compared to `roughness`, but it's an important concept in 3D graphics!\r\n5.  **Mix and Match:** Feel free to apply different `color`, `roughness`, and `metalness` values to all the shapes (`cube`, `sphere`, `cone`, `cylinder`) to create a diverse and interesting scene.",
    "generate_outline": "Students will apply different basic material properties (e.g., color, shininess, basic texture) to various objects within their Web Lab 2 3D scene, observing how materials affect light reflection."
  },
